## Location
pkix-lint/src/deviation.rs:411-433 — serial_lex_ge, serial_lex_le, strip_leading_zeros.

## Finding
The two helpers do near-identical work:

```
fn serial_lex_ge(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> bool {
    let a = strip_leading_zeros(a);
    let b = strip_leading_zeros(b);
    if a.len() != b.len() { return a.len() > b.len(); }
    a >= b
}

fn serial_lex_le(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> bool {
    let a = strip_leading_zeros(a);
    let b = strip_leading_zeros(b);
    if a.len() != b.len() { return a.len() < b.len(); }
    a <= b
}
```

This is the textbook DRY-violation pattern. The idiomatic Rust shape is a single Ordering-returning function:

```
fn serial_cmp(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> core::cmp::Ordering {
    let a = strip_leading_zeros(a);
    let b = strip_leading_zeros(b);
    a.len().cmp(&b.len()).then_with(|| a.cmp(b))
}
```

Call sites then use `.is_ge()` / `.is_le()` on the Ordering value:
```
let serial_ge_start = serial_cmp(serial, start).is_ge();
let serial_le_end = serial_cmp(serial, end).is_le();
```

This:
- Removes the duplicated strip_leading_zeros + length comparison.
- Is the standard idiom for byte-slice numeric comparison.
- Centralises the DER positive-integer ordering rule (length-first, then bytewise) in one place.

## Severity
P4 (opinion / DRY). Both functions are tiny and correct; this is purely a code-cleanliness improvement.

## Tests
The tests at deviation.rs:1066-1090 (serial_lex_ge_basic, serial_lex_le_basic, serial_lex_leading_zeros_stripped) would need to be updated to either:
1. Test serial_cmp directly returning Ordering, or
2. Be renamed and rewritten as boolean-returning thin wrappers around serial_cmp, preserved for source compatibility within the crate.

Either rewriting strategy must keep the same test coverage; no test discipline issue.
